# Line endings, decided by the repository instead of by whichever git the
# contributor happened to install.
#
# Why this file exists, in the concrete: a `windows-latest` CI run failed nine
# tests that pass everywhere else, and seven of them were one cause. Git decides
# line endings at *checkout*, not in the repository, and the default it decides
# with comes from the installer — Git for Windows ships `core.autocrlf=true`,
# and the GitHub Actions Windows runner inherits it. So from one identical
# commit, `src/renderer/shell/shell.css` and `src/renderer/styles/tokens.css`
# arrive as `\r\n` on that machine and `\n` on every machine anybody wrote the
# tests on. Both sheets are parsed as text by tests — there is no CSS parser in
# a Node test and neither test wants one — and a hand-written parser that looks
# for a literal `\n` finds nothing at all in a CRLF file. `shell.test.ts` then
# reported `undefined` for declarations that are plainly in the sheet, and
# `title-bar.test.ts` threw while collecting and never ran a single test.
#
# What this file is NOT: the fix. The parsers were made to tolerate `\r` and
# both test files now feed themselves CRLF input directly, so the bug fails on
# a Mac rather than only on a runner an hour later. That had to happen anyway,
# because `.gitattributes` only governs what *git* writes: a contributor whose
# editor saves CRLF, a file generated by a Windows tool, or anything arriving
# through a path git never touched is unaffected by anything written here. This
# is the belt to that pair of braces — it removes the most common source of the
# input rather than the ability to handle it.

# Everything is a text file stored with LF and checked out with LF, on every
# platform. `text=auto` still lets git detect and leave binaries alone; the
# explicit list below is for the cases where being wrong is expensive.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Batch files are read by `cmd.exe`, which is the one interpreter in this repo
# that genuinely cares: a `.bat` with LF-only endings can mis-parse `goto` and
# multi-line constructs. `-text` rather than `eol=crlf` on purpose — it tells
# git to do no conversion in either direction, so `android/gradlew.bat` keeps
# the exact bytes Gradle shipped it with. `eol=crlf` would first normalise the
# stored blob to LF, which rewrites a vendored file for no benefit.
*.bat -text
*.cmd -text

# Binaries, named rather than detected. `text=auto` guesses by looking for a NUL
# byte in the first 8000, which is usually right — but "usually" applied to an
# icon, a font or a wrapper jar is a corrupted file that only shows up as a
# broken build, and every one of these is tracked here today.
*.png binary
*.ico binary
*.icns binary
*.tiff binary
*.pdf binary
*.jar binary
*.woff2 binary
